  "strategy": "# Approach and rationale\nThe key idea is to use a multi-fidelity optimization approach that gradually improves the sequence using increasingly refined methods. We start by initializing several candidate sequences at different lengths, then iteratively optimize each candidate by combining gradient-based optimization and linear programming techniques. This ensures exploration of large sequences while gradually improving upon the best candidate found so far.\n\nWhy this approach? By using nested optimization techniques, we create a search gradient that systematically explores the space from simple to complex candidates, ensuring we don't get stuck in local minima. Starting with multiple initializations (including parent scaling) and progressively applying tighter constraints allows us to find sequences with lower objectives.\n\n## Methodology\n1. **Initializations**: Sample diverse representatives with 6 different initialization methods (from prior example) including enclosing scales, uniform, exponential, etc.\n2. **Iterative Refinement**: For each candidate, apply a sequence of optimizations with exponentially decreasing relaxation to focus refinement.\n3. **Adaptive Time Allocation**: Use a phase-based system where each step runs to completion or until minimal improvement subsumes further value.\n4. **Resampling & Projection**: Maintain best overall candidate across all scales and time periods.\n\n## Planning\nWe'll use up to 5 minutes to search for candidates. Here's the phased plan:\n- Stage1: Heavy initialization of diverse sequences (4 candidates)\n- Stage2: Apply robust optimization to each (FFT+LP+Peak)\n- Stage3: Secondary optimization to best candidate (gradient+peak)\n- Stage4: Projection to finer resolution (longer sequences) \n- Stage5: LL Java finalization",
